# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (diagnostics & fixed caps)
# Initialized-global-var slot ceiling, cc2 output buffer ceiling, preprocessed
# line-number reporting, the undefined-fn HARD ERROR (default-on since
# v6.3.2 — `--strict` no longer has anything to do with it), the LIVE
# capacity ceilings, and what a v6.4.60+ diagnostic actually looks like.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10 by RUNNING the compiler:
#  · `undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict` was inverted — a reachable undefined fn
#    has been a hard error BY DEFAULT since v6.3.2, `--allow-undef` downgrades
#    it, and `_strict_mode` is now assigned in all 7 forks and read NOWHERE.
#  · the transcripts here put the parenthetical note on its own line; the
#    compiler appends it to the `error:` line. Corrected in both entries.
#  · every capacity figure re-verified against `_capacity_warnings` and the
#    `src/main.cyr` heap map; the code-buffer warning fires at 75 %, not 85 %.
# 6 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "the_gvar_wall"
description = "The initialized global variable limit and how to work within it (refreshed v5.8.47 audit; cap raised pre-v5.8.x)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Re-verified 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10)** against the `src/main.cyr` heap
map — offset and count both unchanged since the v6.4.2 pass below.

**Refreshed 2026-07-04 (v6.4.2 vidya audit)**: cap-bump history.
At cyrius v6.4.2 the gvar_toks table lives at heap offset
**0x729000** and holds **4096 entries** — relocated + grown from
1024@0x198000 at **v6.3.41** (thoth consumer unblock; the old
0x198000 band was FREED and reclaimed for the move, per the
src/main.cyr heap map). Only NON-literal top-level `var = expr;`
inits consume a gvar_toks slot; a bare int-literal `= NUM;` takes
the static-init fast path and enums const-fold — neither counts.
(History: 256 → 1024 across v5.6→v5.8 at 0x198000, → 4096 at
0x729000 at v6.3.41.) The MITIGATION STRATEGIES below are still
load-bearing for sandhi-class consumers + AGNOS, but the wall
itself moved far higher than the original DOOM-era writeup.
Misleading-error-on-undefined-var has long since been fixed
(undefined-var diagnostics name the variable explicitly across
all current backends). Companion entry
`undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict` covers the related
warning-vs-error escalation.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

THE WALL YOU WILL HIT:

The compiler tracks initialized global variables in a table (gvar_toks) at
heap offset 0x98000. The table holds 256 entries. Every `var x = value;` at
file scope consumes one slot. This includes your code AND every library you
include.

When we first tried to compile cyrius-doom with all modules, we thought the
limit was 64. The error message was `unexpected ';'` — completely unhelpful.
It took hours to discover the real cause was a missing variable (removed to
save slots) that another module still referenced.

cc2 v2.2.2 fixed the error message to `undefined variable 'player_ammo_bullets'`
and the fix took 30 seconds.

LESSON: When you hit a mysterious parse error in a large program, check if
you're referencing a variable that doesn't exist. The old compiler gives
misleading errors for this.

MITIGATION STRATEGIES:
1. Use enums for constants — enums don't consume gvar_toks slots:
     enum Screen { SCREEN_WIDTH = 320; SCREEN_HEIGHT = 200; }
   vs:
     var SCREEN_WIDTH = 320;    # costs a slot
     var SCREEN_HEIGHT = 200;   # costs another slot

2. Pack related mutable state into heap blocks:
     var tick_state = 0;   # one slot
     fn tick_init() { tick_state = alloc(24); }
     # [0]=count [8]=start_sec [16]=start_nsec
   vs:
     var tick_count = 0;      # three slots
     var tick_start_sec = 0;
     var tick_start_nsec = 0;

3. Don't include lib/io.cyr if you only need file_open/read/write/close.
   Inline them as one-line functions — saves 9 gvar_toks.

4. Count your globals before adding a new include. The stdlib contributes:
   string.cyr: 0, alloc.cyr: 3, fmt.cyr: 0, io.cyr: 9, args.cyr: 1, vec.cyr: 0

In our DOOM build: 64 globals across 9 modules + 4 stdlib files. Every
single one accounted for.

[[entries]]
name = "the_256kb_ceiling"
description = "Output-buffer ceiling and why static arrays destroy your binary size (refreshed v6.1.27; ceiling raised 256K→2M pre-v5.8.x→16M at v6.1.27; var buf[N] static-array semantics unchanged)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10): the 16 MiB ceiling below is GONE.**
Since **v6.4.52** the output buffer is a **1 GiB off-heap allocation**
on every platform — `src/main.cyr:776` `_output_base =
alloc(1073741824)`, `_output_cap = 1073741824` — not a heap region at
all. There is no 16 MiB output ceiling to hit.

⛔ AND NOTE HOW THIS ENTRY WENT WRONG, because it is the failure this
whole file exists to prevent. An earlier pass on this same day stamped
it "re-verified against the `src/main.cyr` heap map — still 16 MiB,
unchanged". The heap map is a COMMENT, and that comment was itself
stale: it still documented `0x4D9D000 output_buf [16777216]` as live
while nothing had written there since v6.4.52. Verifying a doc against
another doc reproduces the error with a fresh timestamp on it, which is
worse than leaving it unstamped. Read the CODE.

**Refreshed 2026-06-10 (v6.1.27)**: ceiling moved.
At cyrius v6.1.27 the output_buf region is **16 MiB (16,777,216
bytes)** at S+0x4D9D000 (heap-top, per `src/main.cyr` heap map),
not the 256 KB cc2 ceiling described below. History: 1 MB →
2 MB at v5.6.43 (then at S+0x94A000), → 16 MB at v6.1.27 when
phylax hit the 2 MB ELF-output wall pulling bayan (relocated
mid-heap → heap-top; cost is virtual-only, output_buf is never
memset). The DOOM-era "950 KB of static arrays" example would
no longer overflow today, but the underlying RULE — heap-allocate anything
>~100 bytes — remains correct. Companion v5.8.46 fixed the
adjacent token-array cap (262144 → 1048576) which was binding
sandhi consumers like sit; see archived issue
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/archived/2026-04-25-cyrius-fixup-table-cap.md`
for that closeout.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

cc2 caps output at 262,144 bytes (256KB). Both the code buffer and the final
ELF are limited to this size.

Critical: `var buf[N]` allocates N bytes IN THE BINARY. Not on the heap. Not
as BSS. The compiler zero-initializes every byte and writes it to the ELF.

Our first DOOM build had:
  var screen_buf[64000];    # 64KB in binary
  var rgb_buf[256000];      # 256KB — ALONE exceeds the limit
  var map_nodes[114688];    # 112KB
  var map_segs[196608];     # 192KB
  var things[57344];        # 56KB

Total: ~950KB of static arrays. Compiler error: "output too large (3617920/262144)".

THE FIX: Heap allocate everything:
  var screen_buf = 0;       # 8 bytes in binary
  fn framebuf_init() {
      screen_buf = alloc(64000);   # 64KB at runtime
  }

After converting all large arrays to heap: 56KB binary. The data lives in
the 1MB heap that alloc_init() sets up via brk.

RULE: If it's bigger than ~100 bytes, heap-allocate it. Static arrays are
for small stack buffers (var buf[16]) inside functions only.

[[entries]]
name = "the_preprocessed_line_number_problem"
description = "Error messages report preprocessed line numbers, not source file + line — STILL ACTIVE at v5.8.46 (re-confirmed at v5.8.47 vidya audit; partially mitigated by the lex.cyr file_map but not surfaced in the error path)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Status (2026-08-07, v6.5.10 — re-run, unchanged)**: PARTIALLY
MITIGATED. The line number is still the PREPROCESSED-stream line and
the filename still collapses to literal `<source>` — but since v6.4.60
the diagnostic also carries a **column** and prints the **offending
source line with a caret under it**, which in practice removes most of
the counting work below:

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | build/cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add' (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

You can usually just read the excerpt and grep for it. See
`diagnostics_carry_column_excerpt_and_dont_stop_at_the_first_error`
in this file. The underlying file+line tracking is still unshipped.

**Prior status (2026-05-03)**: still active at cyrius v5.8.46.
Empirical re-test: a 5-line program with `var x = SOME_UNDEFINED;`
on line 4 errors as `error:<source>:2: undefined variable
'SOME_UNDEFINED'` — the line number doesn't match the user
source AND the source filename collapses to literal `<source>`
even when invoked through `cyrius build` (which has the real
filename available). Partial mitigation infrastructure exists
(`file_map` + `file_map_str` regions in `src/frontend/lex.cyr`,
ipc'd via `_resolve_file_line` in some error paths) but the
common error sites still emit `error:<source>:N:` with the
preprocessed-stream line number.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

When cc2 reports `error:1984: unexpected ';'`, that 1984 is the line number
in the PREPROCESSED source — after all includes have been expanded into a
single stream.

With 5 stdlib includes (485 lines) + 9 DOOM modules (2500 lines), you get
line numbers in the thousands that don't correspond to any single file.

TO FIND THE REAL LOCATION:
1. Count cumulative lines through your include order
2. Subtract from the error line number
3. That gives you the offset into the specific file

Example: error at preprocessed line 1984
  string.cyr: 85
  alloc.cyr: 103
  fmt.cyr: 216
  args.cyr: 81
  fixed.cyr: 85
  tables.cyr: 114
  wad.cyr: 183
  framebuf.cyr: 149
  map.cyr: 338
  render.cyr: 367
  (cumulative: 1721)
  1984 - 1721 = line 263 in the next file (input.cyr)

This is tedious. The fix is in cc2's roadmap: track source file + line
through preprocessing and report both.

WORKAROUND: Binary search. Comment out includes until the error goes away,
then narrow down. This is faster than counting.

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict"
description = "⚠ INVERTED SINCE v6.3.2 — a REACHABLE undefined-function call is a HARD ERROR by DEFAULT and no binary is emitted; `--allow-undef` downgrades it to a warning. `--strict` is NOT what turns it on any more (and is now read nowhere). Entry name kept for link stability."
category = "cyrius"
---
⚠ **CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10) — the default flipped at v6.3.2 and
this entry taught the OPPOSITE for two minors.** Measured on the live
compiler, no flags:

    $ printf 'var r = bench_print_all();\n' | build/cycc > foo
    warning: undefined function 'bench_print_all'
    warning: undefined function 'bench_print_all' (call site may be unreachable)
    error: refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable undefined function(s) (pass --allow-undef to downgrade)
    $ echo $? ; stat -c%s foo
    1
    0

**No binary. Zero bytes. Exit 1 — by default.** That closes the "ships a
successful build of a crashing binary" class: the old behaviour emitted a
`ud2` / `UDF` at the call site, which SIGILLs at runtime and was
repeatedly misdiagnosed as a toolchain bug.

⛔ **`--strict` IS NOT THE SWITCH, and does nothing here.** The gate reads
a separate `_allow_undef` global (default 0 = hard error), not
`_strict_mode`. Measured: `build/cycc --strict` and `build/cycc` produce
byte-identical output on the case above. `_strict_mode` is still declared
in six of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks (not `main_cx.cyr`) and
assigned by their argv parses, and is **read nowhere** in `src/` — the
remaining mentions are comments. Do not add `--strict` to CI expecting it
to do this; it is already done.

TO OPT OUT (rarely correct):

    cycc --allow-undef      # or --al

Downgrades the error back to a warning and emits the binary. The repo's
own builds use **zero** `--allow-undef`; every reachable undef in the
tree is a real fn via includes, or a documented stub/guard.

⚠ **"REACHABLE" IS LOAD-BEARING.** The check runs AFTER the DCE pass and
consults the reachability bitmap, so a fixup whose host fn is unreachable
from entry warns but does NOT count toward the error (v5.11.59). That is
why you can see a `warning: undefined function 'X'` line and still get a
binary — the two `warning:` lines above are the same symbol reported by
both paths. Top-level / entry-block code is always treated as live.

THE BUG CLASS IT CATCHES is typo-in-fn-name, forgotten include, and API
rename without the caller updated. Surfaced at v5.4.15 when
`bench_print_all` (should have been `bench_report_all`) cost a full debug
cycle because the warning scrolled past in the compile output. Nothing
scrolls past now.

HISTORY, for anyone on an older toolchain: warn-and-emit was the default
through v6.3.1; `--strict` (v5.4.19) was the opt-in hard-fail, and
`cyrius build --strict` forwarded it (v5.11.63). v6.3.2 made the hard
error unconditional and moved the escape hatch to `--allow-undef`.

[[entries]]
name = "live_compiler_capacity_ceilings"
description = "The capacity ceilings a consumer can actually hit — re-verified live at v6.5.10 against `_capacity_warnings` and the src/main.cyr heap map: identifier pool 524288 B (512 KB, v6.4.76), fn_table init 8192 growing to a 32768 hard ceiling, var_table 8192, fixup_table 1048576, string_data 2097152, code buffer 64 MiB growable (512 KiB on cx). Read this instead of any older 262144 / 8192-ceiling figure."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE LIVE NUMBERS (source of truth: `src/common/util.cyr::_capacity_warnings`
plus the `CYRIUS_STATS=1` meter in `src/main.cyr` — go read them, do not
trust a doc that hasn't been premise-checked. Every line below was
re-read out of those two files on 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10; all unchanged
since the v6.4.82 pass):

    fn_table        8192 INITIAL, doubles to a 32768 HARD ceiling
    identifier pool 524288 bytes (512 KB) at heap 0x60000-0xE0000
    var_table       8192
    fixup_table     1048576
    string_data     2097152
    code buffer     67108864 (64 MiB, growable off-heap since v6.4.49)
                    — but only 524288 (512 KiB) on the cx target, which
                      is NOT growable; the warning text differs there
    output buffer   1073741824 (1 GiB, off-heap alloc, since v6.4.52)
    gvar_toks       4096 deferred non-literal global inits, at 0x729000

HOW TO SEE YOUR OWN NUMBERS:

    CYRIUS_STATS=1 cyrius build ...      # full meter on stderr

  Without `CYRIUS_STATS`, the compiler emits an over-cap WARNING per
  table at end of compile. Since v6.4.50 all SEVEN drivers share one
  implementation — before that, only the x86-Linux and Win64 drivers
  warned at all, which is why a codebuf warning would appear on x86 and
  vanish on aarch64/macho/cx for the same program.

  ⚠ The threshold is **85 % for five of the six tables and 75 % for the
  code buffer** — a detail this entry used to flatten into "an 85 %
  warning per table". If you are calibrating against the warning, use
  the right number for the table you are near.

TWO TRAPS IN THE OLDER DOCUMENTATION:

  1. **"identifier buffer 262144"** — retired at v6.4.76, which doubled
     the pool 256 KB → 512 KB in place (into the free 0xA0000-0x100000
     band). LEX's hard guard moved 261872 → **524016**. The pool END
     must stay <= 0x100000: past that physical ceiling the compiler
     HANGS rather than erroring, so this cap cannot simply be doubled
     again in place.

  2. **"fn_table cap 8192"** — 8192 is still the correct INITIAL cap
     (and the var_table cap outright), so most surviving "8192"s in the
     tree are RIGHT. What changed is the ceiling and the warning:
     - v6.2.0 made the fn tables growable (REGFN doubles + rehashes).
     - v6.4.75 fixed a **P0**: six fn-INDEXED side tables had never been
       migrated and were still pinned to fixed 8192-slot bands, so past
       8192 fns index `fi` wrote into slot `fi-8192` of its NEIGHBOUR —
       silent metadata corruption, no diagnostic. All six now lazy-alloc
       at the 32768 max.
     - The WARNING was complicit: it divided by the LIVE `_fnt_cap`, so
       it screamed "99%" at 8191 fns and went **SILENT** the moment the
       table doubled (9000/16384 = 54%) — precisely when the corruption
       began. It now divides by the fixed 32768 ceiling, which does not
       move.
     A blanket 8192 → 32768 find-and-replace INTRODUCES bugs. Check each
     site.

WARN THRESHOLDS (integer arithmetic — `count * 100 / cap >= 85`, and
`>= 75` for the code buffer): fn_table fires at 27853 fns (of 32768);
identifier pool at 445645 bytes (of 524288).

WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HIT ONE: the standing rule is grow the cap, not
trim the program — but the identifier pool no longer has room to grow
in place, and past 32768 fns the honest answer is splitting into
compilation units. The known high-water consumer is **stiva**, which at
v6.4.73 sat ~750 fns below the then-**8192** cliff — i.e. ~7,400 fns,
roughly 23 % of today's 32768 ceiling. ⚠ That distance has NOT been
re-measured since the ceiling moved at v6.4.75; read it as "the biggest
consumer we know of is nowhere near the current ceiling", not as a live
margin.

[[entries]]
name = "diagnostics_carry_column_excerpt_and_dont_stop_at_the_first_error"
description = "Since v6.4.60 every error/warning prints `line:COLUMN` plus the source line with a caret; since v6.4.62 one compile reports MANY errors (panic-mode recovery), writes NO output file if any error fired, and cannot hang on hostile or truncated input."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT YOU SEE NOW (both transcripts re-run at v6.5.10 — the
parenthetical note is APPENDED to the `error:` line, it is not on a
line of its own; an earlier revision of this entry showed it wrapped):

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add' (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

    $ printf 'var a: f64 = f64_from(3);\nvar b = a + 1;\n' | cycc > /dev/null
    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

THREE BEHAVIOURS WORTH RELYING ON (all three re-measured at v6.5.10 —
two missing-`;` errors reported in ONE compile from a two-fn file, a
0-byte output with exit 1, and a source truncated mid-expression
terminating in 2 stderr lines rather than hanging):

  1. **Many errors per compile.** A syntax error puts the parser into
     panic mode; it skips to the next statement boundary and continues.
     One run gives you the batch instead of one error per edit-compile
     cycle. Scope is the `ERR_EXPECT` syntax class (346 sites); about 25
     inline fatal errors still stop on the first hit.
  2. **No output file when any error fired.** Every fork (including cx)
     gates its writer on `_had_error`. A **0-byte output** is the
     failure signature — if you see one, look at stderr, don't debug the
     binary. You can never accidentally run a stale artifact.
  3. **It cannot hang.** A `_had_error`-gated watchdog inside `PEEKT`
     aborts cleanly if the token index stalls past a bound far above any
     legal recursive-descent depth, and `PEEKT` clamps to EOF past the
     token count (v6.4.78). A truncated file used to emit 166,670 error
     lines; it now emits 5. On VALID input none of this machinery runs,
     so there is no cost to correct programs.

READING THE COLUMN: it points at the CURRENT parse token. For a
backward-looking check ("expected `;`") that is the token AFTER the one
you want — trust the caret over the number in those cases. A tab counts
as one column. There is no per-file source line yet (see
`the_preprocessed_line_number_problem`); the excerpt is what makes the
message findable in practice — grep the printed line.

IMPLEMENTATION NOTE (for anyone tempted to "improve" it): the column
costs ZERO new heap — the token byte offset is packed into the HIGH 32
bits of the existing `tok_lines` entry. And the watchdog is cheap for
the same reason the EOF clamp was cheap: `PEEKT` already had an
`_had_error` guard, and the runaway is post-error only, so clamping
inside it measured at −0.07 % on self_compile.
